mihomo: meet Xray's default REALITY client version floor

Switching luci-app-ssr-plus to the mihomo core made every connection fail
with "REALITY authentication failed", while xray-core worked from the same
router, at the same moment, with the same credentials. It also reproduced
on the Windows desktop mihomo, with the panel's own generated config,
against all six nodes -- so it is neither this package, nor the platform,
nor luci-app-ssr-plus's config generation.

REALITY carries a three-byte client version at the start of the ClientHello
session id, and the server compares it against MinClientVer. Since
af7eb68028732a8ee3c0e5d6ab2b8a657bb2e770 (2026-07-11) Xray no longer leaves
that field unset when the config omits it --
infra/conf/transport_security.go:118 substitutes a default:

    } else {
        config.MinClientVer = []byte{26, 3, 27}
    }

mihomo hardcodes 1.8.2 and has done so unchanged, including on the Alpha
branch. REALITY's Value() packs the three bytes big-endian, so 1.8.2 is
67586 against a floor of 1704731: mihomo is refused by every Xray server
that does not explicitly lower minClientVer, which is nearly all of them.
Reporting its own version would not help either -- 1.19.30 is 70430.

Confirmed from the server side. With show:true on the node, every handshake
prints the four values REALITY decides on:

    ClientVer [1 8 2]     conn == conn false
    ClientVer [26 3 27]   conn == conn true

ClientShortId decoded identically, and correctly, in both groups, so the
AEAD opened and config.ShortIds matched; ClientTime was the current second.
tls.go:257 has exactly one gate and only the version term can separate
these two groups. The node's config carries no minClientVer at all, which
is precisely why the default applies.

The floor is not really about versions. It was set from the discussion at
XTLS/Xray-core#6181, where the concern is uTLS fingerprints: clients that
present an outdated Chrome fingerprint, or a current one with
X25519MLKEM768 stripped out, carry a strong distinguishing feature, and the
server refuses them to keep its own address from being classified by it.
So the number asserts "my ClientHello is at least as modern as v26.3.27's".

For mihomo that claim holds only conditionally. component/tls/reality.go:58
removes X25519MLKEM768 from the hello unless support-x25519mlkem768 is set,
which is exactly the modification the discussion calls out. With it on,
mihomo sends an unmodified HelloChrome_Auto and the claim is honest; with
it off, this patch asserts something false about the very property the
floor exists to check. Keep it on.

The floor value itself is written here rather than anything higher, so that
a future increase breaks the handshake immediately and visibly. Headroom
would be worse than useless: the floor rises when a class of fingerprints
becomes detectable, and quietly clearing the new floor would mean going on
using one. The server logs "The default minimal client version is
Xray-core vX.Y.Z" at startup, which is where to look when it breaks.

26.3.27 also stays distinguishable in a shared node log -- this router runs
both cores against the same node, and luci-app-ssr-plus packages xray-core
26.7.28. MaxClientVer has no default, so there is no upper bound.

--- a/component/tls/reality.go
+++ b/component/tls/reality.go
@@ -71,9 +71,9 @@
 		binary.BigEndian.PutUint64(hello.SessionId, uint64(ntp.Now().Unix()))
 
 		copy(hello.SessionId[8:], realityConfig.ShortID[:])
-		hello.SessionId[0] = 1
-		hello.SessionId[1] = 8
-		hello.SessionId[2] = 2
+		hello.SessionId[0] = 26
+		hello.SessionId[1] = 3
+		hello.SessionId[2] = 27
 
 		//log.Debugln("REALITY hello.sessionId[:16]: %v", hello.SessionId[:16])
 
